* 26 Aug 26 Windows port - BOTH OF THESE READ A VOC RECORD NOW, AND NEITHER
*   SHELLS OUT.  Owner's ruling: the text CONFIG shows should be internal to SD
*   rather than a file it reaches out to.
*
*   WHAT THEY USED TO BE:  execute "!less " : @sdsys:@ds:'licence'
*
*   THAT FAILED TWICE OVER ON WINDOWS and either half alone was enough.
*   There is no "less" on a Windows machine and the install ships none - so
*   the pager was never going to run.  And "!" is CPROC's shell escape, gated
*   on OS.USERS field 1 (op_sh.c os_permitted), so an ordinary user was
*   refused before that even mattered - with a message about the operating
*   system shell, naming neither CONFIG nor the licence.  Displaying a licence
*   must not require shell permission.
*
*   THE RECORDS ARE IN SDSYS's OWN VOC AND NOT IN NEWVOC.  voc_template is
*   the administrative superset that becomes SDSYS's VOC; newvoc is what
*   CREATEA copies into every account.  The licence is 44 KB, so in newvoc it
*   would land in every account's VOC and be copied again by every
*   UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.  gplbld/mkvocdoc.py generates both records FROM the files
*   that still ship in the data tree, so the two cannot drift; --check asserts
*   it.
*
*   THE FILES STILL SHIP, and that is not redundancy: SD is GPL-3,
*   sdsys/licence is the only copy of the licence in the distribution, and
*   sd.iss has no LicenseFile - so a GPL program with no readable licence for
*   anyone who has not started SD is what deleting it would produce.

show.contrib:
   * added contributor display (dsm) 2024-01-22
   doc.name = '$contrib'
   gosub show.doc
return

show.gpl:
   doc.name = '$licence'
   gosub show.doc
return

* ----------------------------------------------------------------------
* show.doc  -  print an X-type record from SDSYS's VOC, a field to a line.
*
* SDSYS's VOC RATHER THAN THIS ACCOUNT'S, so there is one copy on the system
* and it is reachable from wherever CONFIG was typed.  The same @sdsys route
* LOGIN uses for batch.jobs.
*
* PAGINATION IS SD's OWN.  Turning it on for the duration is what replaces the
* pager, and it is put back afterwards whichever way this leaves - a verb that
* silently changed the session's paging would be a worse bug than the one
* being fixed.
* ----------------------------------------------------------------------

show.doc:
   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'voc' to sdsys.voc else
      display 'Cannot open the SDSYS VOC, so ' : doc.name : ' cannot be shown.'
      return
   end

   read doc.rec from sdsys.voc, doc.name else
      close sdsys.voc
      display doc.name : ' is not in the SDSYS VOC.'
      return
   end
   close sdsys.voc

   doc.lines = dcount(doc.rec, @fm)
   if doc.lines < 2 then
      display doc.name : ' holds no text.'
      return
   end

*  THE SAME PAGINATION IDIOM CPROC USES (CPROC:525), not SETPU: SETPU takes a
*  numeric PU$ key and a unit number and is for print units, while pagination
*  is a kernel setting.  Read it, turn it on, put it back.
   pagination.state = kernel(K$PAGINATE, -1)
   i = kernel(K$PAGINATE, @true)

   crt @(-1) :
*  Field 1 is the record type, X.  The text starts at field 2.
   for doc.i = 2 to doc.lines
      print doc.rec<doc.i>
   next doc.i

   i = kernel(K$PAGINATE, pagination.state)
return

